---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const updated = 'August 2026';
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy · Warren Labs"
  description="How Warren Labs and the Attune macOS app handle your data. We never collect anything without asking, and we never collect who you are or what you listen to."
>
  <Nav variant="home" />
  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">Privacy</span>
      <h2>Privacy Policy</h2>
      <p class="sec-lede">
        <b>We never collect anything without asking, and we never collect who you are or what you
        listen to.</b> Right now the Attune app collects nothing at all, and this website stores an
        email address only if you choose to give us one.
      </p>

      <h3>What we will never collect</h3>
      <p>
        These are not settings you can turn on. We do not collect them and do not intend to build the
        ability to: your identity, your contact details, your listening history, the contents of your
        music library, your file paths, or anything else that could be tied back to you as a person.
        A tuning you make is yours; what you play through it is not our business.
      </p>

      <h3>Attune (the macOS app)</h3>
      <p>
        <b>Attune currently collects, stores, transmits, and shares nothing.</b> It runs
        entirely on your Mac, has no network access, and contains no analytics, telemetry, or
        tracking of any kind. Audio files you choose are read locally only to play them — nothing
        about your music library, your listening, or your tunings ever leaves your device.
      </p>

      <h3>The one thing that can leave</h3>
      <p>
        Something you send yourself. If you use <b>Have feedback?</b>, the app writes a message and
        opens it in <em>your</em> mail client, with the technical context it attaches — app version,
        macOS version, output device, which headphone pair is loaded — shown to you in the window
        before you send it. You send it; we don't.
      </p>

      <h3>If that ever changes</h3>
      <p>
        We may one day want crash reports, so bugs get fixed without someone having to write in, or
        anonymous results from the listening exam, so the house target improves for everyone. If we
        build either, all of the following are binding: it is <b>off by default</b>; we ask once, in
        plain language, and say what it's for; <b>you can see the exact payload</b> before you agree
        and any time after; one click turns it off permanently; and <b>this page is updated first</b>,
        before the feature ships, never after. Still nothing from the list above, and still nothing
        tied to you. A pre-ticked box, a consent bundled into something else, or a friendly dialog
        that defaults to yes would all break that promise, so we won't use them.
      </p>

      <h3>This website (warrenlabs.com)</h3>
      <p>
        If you enter your email address in a signup, waitlist, measurement-request or feedback form,
        we use it only to reply and to contact you about Warren Labs. It is stored by our form
        provider (Formspree) and by us, and is never sold or shared with advertisers. Ask us to
        delete it at any time. The site sets no advertising or cross-site tracking cookies. The
        feedback form attaches the page you were on, your window size and your browser version —
        shown to you before you send — and email is optional there.
      </p>

      <h3>The measurement service</h3>
      <p>
        If you send a headphone in, we need your name and address to ship it back, and we keep them
        for that. Published measurements carry a credit line only in the form you choose: your name,
        a handle, or nothing at all.
      </p>

      <h3>Why it's written this way</h3>
      <p>
        We don't sell headphones and we take no commission on any you buy, so unlike a shop we have
        no reason to want a profile of you. Not collecting it isn't a sacrifice on our side; it's
        just what the business doesn't need.
      </p>

      <h3>Contact</h3>
      <p>
        Questions, or a deletion request: <a href="mailto:jamey@warrenlabs.com">jamey@warrenlabs.com</a>.
      </p>

      <p class="updated">Last updated: {updated}</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Footer variant="home" />
</Layout>

<style>
  .prose { max-width: 720px; }
  .prose h3 { margin: 30px 0 8px; font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .prose p { margin: 0 0 14px; line-height: 1.65; }
  .prose .updated { margin-top: 30px; font-size: 0.85rem; opacity: 0.55; }
</style>
